<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Louis I of Hungary</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1326">1326</a>–<a href="/years/1382">1382</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Louis the Great · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capetian-house-of-anjou">Capetian House of Anjou</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Louis I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, known as Louis the Great, was king of Hungary and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a> from 1342 and king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> from 1370 until his death in 1382. A member of the Angevin dynasty, he forced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> to give up <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalmatia">Dalmatia</a> in 1358 and made Hungary a leading power of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. His daughters Mary and Jadwiga succeeded him in Hungary and Poland.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/louis-i-of-hungary.jpg" alt="Louis I of Hungary" width="320" height="486" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Márk Kálti · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Louis_I_(Chronicon_Pictum).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under <b>Louis I</b> they campaigned widely: in Naples in 1347–1350 to avenge the murder of his brother Andrew, against Venice, which ceded Dalmatia in the Treaty of Zadar in 1358, and in the Balkans, where Bosnia, Serbian lords, Wallachia and Moldavia were drawn into Hungarian suzerainty.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-hungary-1301-1526">Kingdom of Hungary (1301-1526) · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>According to the traditional account, around 1346–1347 King <b>Louis I of Hungary</b> sent Dragoș, a Romanian noble from Maramureș, across the mountains to found a frontier march on the Moldova River; the exact date and circumstances are uncertain, and the tradition of his “dismounting” (descălecat) was elaborated later.</blockquote><a href="/empire/principality-of-moldavia">Principality of Moldavia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>When Venice lost its war against <b>Louis I of Hungary</b>, the Treaty of Zadar of 1358 forced it to give up all of Dalmatia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-ragusa">Republic of Ragusa · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Jadwiga, youngest daughter of <b>Louis I of Hungary</b>, was crowned king of Poland, rather than queen, to highlight her sovereign status.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-16">16 October in history</a></li><li><blockquote>After <b>Louis I of Hungary</b> died without a male heir, his daughter Mary was crowned with the title of &quot;King&quot; of Hungary.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/september-17">17 September in history</a></li><li><blockquote>As the Serbian Empire disintegrated, the city accepted the protection of <b>Louis I of Hungary</b> in 1371 and ran its own affairs.</blockquote><a href="/state/commune-of-kotor">Commune of Kotor</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/visegrad">Visegrád</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/hungary">Hungary</a>. Died in Trnava, in what is now <a href="/countries/slovakia">Slovakia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=47.79,18.97&amp;year=1362" aria-label="Visegrád on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4992 490 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Louis I of Hungary"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5442 760h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5442 760h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=47.79,18.97&amp;year=1362">What was here in 1362? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
